{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NNvZjhq2DNz9vRrzjLfV42","type":"tool_result","content":"412\t/// callback (see [`StreamDataCallback`]).  Identifies which sort of\n413\t/// notification the stack is delivering — stream data, lifecycle\n414\t/// transition, datagram event, path event, etc.\n415\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n416\tpub enum CallbackEvent {\n417\t    StreamData = 0,\n418\t    StreamFin,\n419\t    StreamReset,\n420\t    StopSending,\n421\t    StatelessReset,\n422\t    Close,\n423\t    ApplicationClose,\n424\t    StreamGap,\n425\t    PrepareToSend,\n426\t    AlmostReady,\n427\t    Ready,\n428\t    Datagram,\n429\t    VersionNegotiation,\n430\t    RequestAlpnList,\n431\t    SetAlpn,\n432\t    PacingChanged,\n433\t    PrepareDatagram,\n434\t    DatagramAcked,\n435\t    DatagramLost,\n436\t    DatagramSpurious,\n437\t    PathAvailable,\n438\t    PathSuspended,\n439\t    PathDeleted,\n440\t    PathQualityChanged,\n441\t    PathAddressObserved,\n442\t    AppWakeup,\n443\t    NextPathAllowed,\n444\t}\n445\t\n446\t// Transport-parameter shapes live in [`crate::tp`].\n447\t\n448\t// Stream-id decomposition lives on [`crate::stream::StreamId`].\n449\t\n450\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n451\t// Time management.\n452\t\n453\t/// Wall-clock microseconds since the Unix epoch.\n454\t///\n455\t/// The C body reads the OS clock; in Rust this requires the `std`\n456\t/// feature (`std::time::SystemTime`).  The `cfg`-gated split lands\n457\t/// when the crate gains its `std` feature and the rest of the\n458\t/// `no_std + alloc` plumbing — for Phase 1 the function is just a\n459\t/// `todo!()` stub.\n460\tpub fn current_time() -> u64 {\n461\t    todo!()\n462\t}\n463\t\n464\timpl Quic {\n465\t    /// Virtual time used by this QUIC context (wall-clock or\n466\t    /// simulated, depending on whether a simulated-time pointer was\n467\t    /// supplied at creation).  C: