{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCGxWcH13LgrYZExDBMC2o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Test cases for `picoquictest/cnxstress.c`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Exercises connection-level stress — multiple parallel client connections,\n4\t//! message passing, connection teardown — and connection-limit enforcement.\n5\t//!\n6\t//! The stress context creates a server `Quic`, a client `Quic`, and two\n7\t//! simulated links.  A loop drives connection setup, message exchange, and\n8\t//! teardown.  `cnx_limit` verifies that the stack sends `SERVER_BUSY` when\n9\t//! the connection table is full.\n10\t\n11\tuse std::cell::RefCell;\n12\tuse std::rc::Rc;\n13\t\n14\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n15\t\n16\tuse crate::errors::TransportError;\n17\tuse crate::tests::util::{\n18\t    TEST_FILE_SERVER_CERT, TEST_FILE_SERVER_KEY, TestSimLink, TestSimPacket, test_random,\n19\t};\n20\tuse crate::tp::TransportParameters;\n21\tuse crate::{\n22\t    CallbackEvent, Connection, ConnectionId, Duration, Error, Instant, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE,\n23\t    State, StreamDataCallback, current_time,\n24\t};\n25\t\n26\tconst CNX_STRESS_ALPN: &str = \"cnxstress\";\n27\t\n28\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n29\t// Stream context.\n30\t\n31\t/// Per-stream state for both sending and receiving sides.\n32\t/// C: `cnx_stress_stream_ctx_t`.\n33\t#[allow(dead_code)]\n34\tstruct CnxStressStreamCtx {\n35\t    stream_id: u64,\n36\t    /// Timestamp embedded in the first 8 bytes of the message payload.\n37\t    send_time: u64,\n38\t    /// Total message size encoded in bytes 8–15.\n39\t    nb_bytes_expected: u64,\n40\t    nb_bytes_received: u64,\n41\t    nb_bytes_sent: u64,\n42\t}\n43\t\n44\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n45\t// Shared state (callback ↔ outer loop).\n46\t\n47\t/// Fields that both the connection callbacks and the outer simulation loop\n48\t/// read or write.  Wrapped in `Rc<RefCell<>>` so callbacks can hold a clone.\n49\t/// C: the callback-mutated subset of `cnx_stress_ctx_t`.\n50\tstruct CnxStressShared {\n51\t    nb_clients: usize,\n52\t    nb_servers: usize,\n53\t    nb_clients_deleted: usize,\n54\t    /// `c_active[rank]` is `true` while the client connection at that slot\n55\t    /// is live.  Set to `false` in the close/application_close callback.\n56\t    c_active: Vec<bool>,\n57\t    /// Same for server-side connections.\n58\t    s_active: Vec<bool>,\n59\t    is_limit_test: bool,\n60\t    limit_test_got_server_busy: bool,\n61\t    nb_client_target: usize,\n62\t    nb_messages_target: usize,\n63\t    message_size: u64,\n64\t    nb_messages_sent: usize,\n65\t    nb_messages_errors: usize,\n66\t    nb_messages_received: usize,\n67\t    sum_message_delays: i64,\n68\t    sum_square_message_delays: f64,\n69\t    message_delay_min: i64,\n70\t    message_delay_max: i64,\n71\t    /// Mirrors `CnxStressCtx::simulated_time`; updated each loop iteration so\n72\t    /// callbacks can compute `time_now – send_time` without access to the\n73\t    /// outer context.  C: obtained via `picoquic_get_quic_time(cnx->quic)`.\n74\t    simulated_time: u64,\n75\t}\n76\t\n77\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n78\t// Outer simulation loop context.\n79\t\n80\t/// Full simulation state.  C: `cnx_stress_ctx_t`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"483d188f-86bb-48b4-b530-27cbba01828e","uuid":"f78cdf5a-3e25-4e88-a67c-f9b4759e6065","timestamp":"2026-05-05T03:40:22.989Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tests/cnxstress.rs","content":"//! Test cases for `picoquictest/cnxstress.c`.\n//!\n//! Exercises connection-level stress — multiple parallel client connections,\n//! message passing, connection teardown — and connection-limit enforcement.\n//!\n//! The stress context creates a server `Quic`, a client `Quic`, and two\n//! simulated links.  A loop drives connection setup, message exchange, and\n//! teardown.  `cnx_limit` verifies that the stack sends `SERVER_BUSY` when\n//! the connection table is full.\n\nuse std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::rc::Rc;\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::errors::TransportError;\nuse crate::tests::util::{\n    TEST_FILE_SERVER_CERT, TEST_FILE_SERVER_KEY, TestSimLink, TestSimPacket, test_random,\n};\nuse crate::tp::TransportParameters;\nuse crate::{\n    CallbackEvent, Connection, ConnectionId, Duration, Error, Instant, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE,\n    State, StreamDataCallback, current_time,\n};\n\nconst CNX_STRESS_ALPN: &str = \"cnxstress\";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Stream context.\n\n/// Per-stream state for both sending and receiving sides.\n/// C: `cnx_stress_stream_ctx_t`.\n#[allow(dead_code)]\nstruct CnxStressStreamCtx {\n    stream_id: u64,\n    /// Timestamp embedded in the first 8 bytes of the message payload.\n    send_time: u64,\n    /// Total message size encoded in bytes 8–15.\n    nb_bytes_expected: u64,\n    nb_bytes_received: u64,\n    nb_bytes_sent: u64,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Shared state (callback ↔ outer loop).\n\n/// Fields that both the connection callbacks and the outer simulation loop\n/// read or write.  Wrapped in `Rc<RefCell<>>` so callbacks can hold a clone.\n/// C: the callback-mutated subset of `cnx_stress_ctx_t`.\nstruct CnxStressShared {\n    nb_clients: usize,\n    nb_servers: usize,\n    nb_clients_deleted: usize,\n    /// `c_active[rank]` is `true` while the client connection at that slot\n    /// is live.  Set to `false` in the close/application_close callback.\n    c_active: Vec<bool>,\n    /// Same for server-side connections.\n    s_active: Vec<bool>,\n    is_limit_test: bool,\n    limit_test_got_server_busy: bool,\n    nb_client_target: usize,\n    nb_messages_target: usize,\n    message_size: u64,\n    nb_messages_sent: usize,\n    nb_messages_errors: usize,\n    nb_messages_received: usize,\n    sum_message_delays: i64,\n    sum_square_message_delays: f64,\n    message_delay_min: i64,\n    message_delay_max: i64,\n    /// Mirrors `CnxStressCtx::simulated_time`; updated each loop iteration so\n    /// callbacks can compute `time_now – send_time` without access to the\n    /// outer context.  C: obtained via `picoquic_get_quic_time(cnx->quic)`.\n    simulated_time: u64,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Outer simulation loop context.\n\n/// Full simulation state.  C: `cnx_stress_ctx_t`.","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":921}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01XqC9odrdTv8NvcjBwJ6Fxb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see - the `cnxstress.rs` already uses `Rc<RefCell<>>` for shared state, and also shows that `simulated_time` is stored in the shared context as a `u64` (since there's no direct access to quic time from within a callback).\n\nThis confirms the approach. The cnxstress test also uses `PrepareToSend` but stores `simulated_time` explicitly in the shared context. Similarly, in the flow_control test, I need to store simulated_time in the shared context.\n\nNow let me look at how cplusplus handles the StreamDataCallback (probably the test_api callback in cnxstress):","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1543,"cache_read_input_tokens":122427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1543},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"483d188f-86bb-48b4-b530-27cbba01828e","uuid":"ac9e1c88-0594-4a83-b386-c3299d9694a5"}
